1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,053
This is ChainThread, built by Najia Pan, a research desk for Ethereum investigations.

2
00:00:06,053 --> 00:00:14,900
It remembers the evidence standard a researcher approved, so the next review can use that standard alongside the transactions.

3
00:00:14,900 --> 00:00:20,487
This prototype uses real Zetaris data, H-MEM, and live NVIDIA Nemotron calls.

4
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:27,286
Here, I paused because a few transfers do not establish a sustained relationship.

5
00:00:27,286 --> 00:00:30,132
My standard is continued interaction over time.

6
00:00:30,132 --> 00:00:35,825
I have not chosen a numeric threshold, and the system should not invent one.

7
00:00:35,825 --> 00:00:39,484
Only this explicit confirmation writes the note into H-MEM.

8
00:00:47,000 --> 00:00:51,949
These five records came from an actual Zetaris query of Ethereum transactions.

9
00:00:51,949 --> 00:00:56,898
Two belong to the loaded baseline, and three to the observation window.

10
00:00:56,898 --> 00:00:59,785
One recipient receives twice, forty-one minutes apart.

11
00:00:59,785 --> 00:01:03,909
The app shows the historical query snapshot and its SQL.

12
00:01:03,909 --> 00:01:08,033
It does not claim these recipients are new to Ethereum.

13
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:26,273
The server retrieves my confirmed record through H-MEM and sends it with these transactions to Nemotron.

14
00:01:26,273 --> 00:01:28,626
This is an actual model request.

15
00:01:28,626 --> 00:01:30,586
Its answer remains a draft.

16
00:01:30,586 --> 00:01:39,212
The linked transaction hashes help me inspect the factual support, but a valid citation does not guarantee that the interpretation is correct.

17
00:02:05,000 --> 00:02:11,449
Here I keep the judgment inconclusive, and I correct an error in the model's window description.

18
00:02:11,449 --> 00:02:15,076
The repeated recipient appears only in the observation window.

19
00:02:15,076 --> 00:02:19,107
After reviewing the table, I explicitly save the revised note.

20
00:02:19,107 --> 00:02:21,122
The original stays in history.

21
00:02:21,122 --> 00:02:24,347
The AI draft never becomes a judgment automatically.

22
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:32,359
After refresh, the revised note remains.

23
00:02:32,359 --> 00:02:39,044
The app identifies an analysis that used an earlier note, so I know when to rerun it.

24
00:02:39,044 --> 00:02:44,549
I can export the transactions, confirmed versions and model drafts as a research record.

25
00:02:44,549 --> 00:02:46,516
My API key is excluded.

26
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:01,826
Zetaris supplies the evidence.

27
00:03:01,826 --> 00:03:06,393
H-MEM stores and retrieves the confirmed standard and revision history.

28
00:03:06,393 --> 00:03:08,219
Nemotron drafts the check.

29
00:03:08,219 --> 00:03:09,589
The researcher decides.

30
00:03:09,589 --> 00:03:16,895
We found a model error during testing, so citation links alone do not certify an interpretation.

31
00:03:16,895 --> 00:03:18,721
Human review remains essential.

32
00:03:28,000 --> 00:03:31,845
This is a local prototype using a historical data snapshot.

33
00:03:31,845 --> 00:03:36,074
Nemotron currently runs through NVIDIA directly, and Nightshift is not integrated.

34
00:03:36,074 --> 00:03:39,149
We have not proved an advantage over Project.

35
00:03:39,149 --> 00:03:46,839
The next test is whether an analyst can prepare and hand off a more reviewable research note with less effort.
